!
! Lines 49 and 50 print NO BINARY, exactly as p.37's lines 42-43 and
! pp.9-12's message rows do, so NO CHARACTER CODE IS CHECKED OR CAN
! BE.  What IS checked is the COUNTING: eight 8-bit characters is
! one word each, and line 51's S END CM lands on 215016.00 only if
! both rows are exactly that long.  That also settles line 50's
! blanks WITHOUT measuring them -- "  /  /63" is the only reading
! with eight characters; three blanks in each gap would be ten and
! would push S END CM to 215017.00, which the page does not print.
!
! >>> WHAT THE ORACLE DOES NOT CHECK. <<<  The DATA DESCRIPTIONS
! the SYN rows print in the BINARY OUTPUT column -- "BU,01 ,10",
! "BU,02 ,10", "BU,22 ,10", "BU,100,10" -- are NOT checked by
! listing_oracle, which has no renderer for them (the same is true
! of printed p.98's SYN rows).  The !SYM lines below check each
! SYN's ADDRESS and nothing else, so a SYN(BU,2) written as
! SYN(BU,1) would pass.  The lengths were transcribed from the
! column and are recorded in the listing block; they are not
! independently verified.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER LIMITATION
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! strap_asm does not implement the INDMK pseudo-op (line 53).  The
! deck writes a bit-identical DD(BU,64) in its place and keeps the
! printed form in the row's !CHECK note; see the comment above the
! statement.  NOTHING UNDER strap/ WAS MODIFIED -- this is reported,
! not fixed.  C22-6729's own pseudo-op list (OCR line 3711) groups
! INDMK with RF, VF, CW and CNOP, all of which strap_asm already
! has, so it is a one-pseudo-op gap and not a design question.

